  "title": "Discovery Engine: Model Armor Dispute Feedback & Granular User Signals",
  "summary": "Discovery Engine now allows developers to capture detailed user disputes for Model Armor verdicts, enabling better refinement of AI safety features. Additionally, new positive feedback reasons provide more nuanced insights into user satisfaction.",
  "details": "Developers can now integrate more deeply with Model Armor's feedback loop. The `GoogleCloudDiscoveryengineV1Feedback` schema gains an optional `modelArmorDisputeInfo` field, referencing the new `GoogleCloudDiscoveryengineV1FeedbackModelArmorDisputeInfo` schema. This allows recording user-provided `expectedLabel`, raw `sanitizationResultJson`, and the `templateName` when a user disputes a `BLOCK` or `FLAG` verdict, crucial for improving AI moderation accuracy.\n\nFeedback granularity is also enhanced with new positive `reasons` in `GoogleCloudDiscoveryengineV1Feedback`: `RELEVANT`, `COMPREHENSIVE`, `GOOD_CITATION`, and `ACCURATE`. These provide richer signals for evaluating search and recommendation quality.\n\nFor project provisioning, `GoogleCloudDiscoveryengineV1ProvisionProjectRequest` now accepts an optional `idpConfig` to set up identity provider configurations and ACLs during creation.\n\nFinally, new schemas `ApiservingMcpMcpToolVisibility` and `ApiservingMcpMcpToolVisibilityFieldVisibility` have been added, seemingly related to internal tool visibility restrictions. Note that the `visibilityEnforcementStrategy` within these schemas is explicitly marked as 'DO NOT USE' and 'not yet implemented', indicating they are likely for internal or very early preview use.",
